===Verb===
'''[[intentional]] [[walk]]'''

# {{baseball|rare|or|nonstandard}} To [[intentionally walk]] (a batter): to give (a batter) an [[#Noun|intentional walk]].
#* '''1991''' July 17, "[http://groups.google.com/group/biz.clarinet.sample/msg/fb928955df2d7e62?q=intentional-walked DiMaggio recalls the day ``the streak&#39;' ended]", United Press International:
#*: A pitcher by the name of Johnny Babich '''intentional walked''' DiMaggio in his first at-bat and tried again in his second.
#* '''1994''' May 5, Jonathan Schmitt, "[http://groups.google.com/group/rec.sport.baseball/msg/17dc520337b6f168?q=intentional-walked Re: Pedro Martinez: Dead by dawn?]", in <tt>rec.sport.baseball</tt>, ''Usenet'':
#*: He '''intentional walked''' him, that means he had no problems with loading the bases and facing Dan Wilson.  Wilson is batting around 150.  There were two outs.  He '''intentional walked''' Anthony.
#* '''2006''' October 3, Luther Root, "[http://groups.google.com/group/alt.sports.baseball.ny-mets/msg/7dceb3e4f3ffc60c?q=intentional-walked Re: Kenny Rogers]", in <tt>alt.sports.baseball.ny-mets</tt>, ''Usenet'':
#*: I just double-checked the box score; I always believed Rogers only '''intentional walked''' the first hitter he faced (perhaps a repressed memory of a traumatic experience).

